# Sgr A* Non-Commercial Source Licence v1.0

**Version 1.0 — 22 September 2026**

**Copyright © 2026 Mark Hurrell. All rights reserved except those expressly granted by this licence.**

## 1. Purpose

This licence permits the Sgr A* Simulator to be studied, used, tested, modified, demonstrated and shared for genuine non-commercial purposes while preserving the authorship, provenance and non-commercial status of the original work.

The project is intended to support learning, teaching, student development, academic and scientific research, public engagement and non-commercial experimentation.

Commercial exploitation of the Software, whether direct or indirect, is not permitted without a separate written licence from the Copyright Holder.

Providing the Software without charge does not, by itself, make a use non-commercial.

## 2. Definitions

For the purposes of this licence:

**“Software”** means the original source code, executable or browser-delivered code, scripts, user-interface code, configuration files and other original software material created for the Sgr A* Simulator and distributed by the Copyright Holder under this licence.

**“Project Content”** means original project documentation, explanatory text, original graphics, original reference material and other non-software material that is expressly distributed under this licence.

**“Covered Material”** means the Software and any Project Content expressly distributed under this licence.

Covered Material does **not** include third-party libraries, dependencies, assets, code, data or other material merely packaged, linked, referenced or distributed with the project. Third-party material remains governed by its own licence.

**“Copyright Holder”** means Mark Hurrell in relation to material for which he owns, or is authorised to license, the relevant rights.

**“You”** means the individual or legal entity exercising rights under this licence.

**“Modified Work”** means a work that modifies, adapts, translates, extends or otherwise derives from copyrightable expression contained in Covered Material.

**“Commercial Use”** means use undertaken for, on behalf of, or primarily directed toward obtaining commercial advantage, revenue, monetary compensation, business benefit or another material commercial benefit.

A use does not become Commercial Use merely because it later has an incidental, unforeseen or remote beneficial consequence.

**“Non-Commercial Use”** means use undertaken without a commercial purpose or intended commercial advantage, including the educational, academic and research activities expressly permitted by this licence.

**“Generated Output”** means an image, animation, plot, numerical result, simulation result, recording, export or other material produced through operation of the Software.

**“Corresponding Source”** means the preferred source form and associated material reasonably necessary to understand, modify and build the version of the Software or Modified Work being provided or operated.

## 3. General Permission

Subject to all conditions of this licence, You may:

a. run the Software for personal and private purposes;

b. inspect and study its source code;

c. use it for learning, experimentation and hobby activity;

d. use it for genuine academic, scientific and educational purposes as permitted by Section 4;

e. demonstrate the Software for permitted educational, academic, scientific or non-commercial purposes;

f. modify the Software for permitted Non-Commercial Uses;

g. create Modified Works for permitted Non-Commercial Uses;

h. reproduce Project Content for permitted educational, academic, scientific or non-commercial purposes, subject to attribution;

i. redistribute the Software, Project Content or Modified Works without charge, subject to this licence; and

j. publish results, criticism, benchmarks and research arising from permitted use.

These permissions apply only to Non-Commercial Use.

## 4. Education, Academic Research and Demonstration

The purpose of this section is to permit broad use of the project in genuine education, student development, academic research, scientific communication and public engagement without those activities becoming prohibited merely because they take place within a funded institution.

### 4.1 Permitted Educational and Academic Use

A school, college, university, public research institution, charity, museum, observatory or other educational or research body may use the Covered Material for genuine educational, academic or scientific purposes.

Permitted activities include:

a. classroom and laboratory teaching;

b. lectures, seminars, workshops and tutorials;

c. student exercises, assignments and projects;

d. undergraduate and postgraduate research;

e. dissertations and theses;

f. doctoral research;

g. academic research projects;

h. demonstrations of scientific or technical work;

i. demonstrations at conferences, seminars, research meetings and academic events;

j. university open days and educational outreach;

k. public-engagement and science-communication events;

l. museum, observatory and educational demonstrations;

m. research presentations and posters;

n. academic publications;

o. teaching notes, lecture slides, worksheets and course materials;

p. recorded lectures and educational videos;

q. screenshots, plots, animations, simulation recordings and other Generated Outputs used to explain the Software or the science demonstrated through it;

r. research-group and institutional websites describing or demonstrating genuine academic work;

s. student portfolios and demonstrations of work undertaken during study; and

t. reasonable modification of the Software for any of the above purposes.

### 4.2 Ordinary Institutional Funding Does Not Make Use Commercial

The following do **not**, by themselves, make an otherwise permitted educational or academic use Commercial Use:

a. tuition fees paid to an educational institution;

b. salaries paid to lecturers, teachers, researchers, technicians or other staff;

c. ordinary student fees;

d. public research grants;

e. charitable research grants;

f. research-council funding;

g. scholarships, bursaries and studentships;

h. ordinary institutional funding;

i. payment of conference or event attendance fees;

j. recovery of reasonable costs associated with education, research or an academic event;

k. publication fees or ordinary academic publishing arrangements;

l. institutional promotion of genuine teaching or research activity; or

m. use of a general-purpose commercial hosting, video, repository or communications platform.

Accordingly, a university does not require a commercial licence merely because it charges tuition, employs paid staff, receives research funding, or uses the Software as part of ordinary teaching or academic research.

### 4.3 Demonstrations and Teaching Materials

Covered Material and Generated Outputs may be shown, reproduced and incorporated into genuine educational, academic and scientific materials.

This includes use in lectures and presentations, course materials, academic papers, dissertations and theses, conference presentations, educational websites, recorded teaching, student portfolios, public-engagement activities, research demonstrations and non-commercial scientific communication.

Where reasonably practicable, public material demonstrating the Software itself should acknowledge:

> **Sgr A* Simulator — original software and project by Mark Hurrell. Copyright © 2026 Mark Hurrell.**

Failure to place attribution on every individual plot, screenshot or frame does not by itself constitute a licence violation where appropriate attribution is provided in the surrounding work, credits, acknowledgements or accompanying material.

### 4.4 Recorded and Online Education

Permitted educational activity may be delivered in person or remotely.

The Software and Generated Outputs may therefore be used in live online teaching, university virtual-learning environments, recorded lectures, educational livestreams, publicly accessible academic demonstrations and non-commercial educational videos.

The mere appearance of general advertising inserted by a third-party platform does not make the educator's use Commercial Use where the educator does not receive advertising, sponsorship or other commercial revenue connected with that use.

### 4.5 Student Rights

Students may use, study, modify and demonstrate the Software as part of their education and academic development.

Students may include work created using the Software in coursework, dissertations and theses, portfolios, academic applications, presentations, research demonstrations, job applications and interviews, and demonstrations of their own skills and work.

A student does not breach this licence merely because educational experience gained using the Software later assists that student in obtaining employment or pursuing a career.

### 4.6 Boundary With Commercial Activity

The permissions in this section do not extend to activity whose primary purpose is commercial exploitation.

A separate commercial licence is required where, for example, the Software is used:

a. to provide commercial consultancy;

b. to perform contract research primarily for a commercial client;

c. to develop a commercial product or service;

d. by a university spin-out, trading subsidiary or other commercial company for its business activities;

e. in a paid corporate training product;

f. in commercial professional-development or executive-training services supplied to external customers;

g. to produce proprietary deliverables for a commercial customer;

h. to provide a commercial hosted service;

i. as part of advertising, sponsorship or promotional activity undertaken primarily for commercial gain; or

j. in research whose principal purpose is to provide a specific commercial organisation with proprietary product-development or competitive advantage.

The existence of an industry partner or commercial sponsor does not automatically make an entire academic project prohibited. The relevant question is whether the particular use of the Software is genuinely academic or educational, or is instead being undertaken principally as commercial work for the benefit of that organisation.

### 4.7 Mixed Academic and Commercial Projects

Where a project contains both academic and commercial activities, academic participants may continue to exercise the educational and research permissions granted by this section for genuinely academic portions of the project.

Those permissions do not automatically extend to a commercial partner.

A commercial organisation wishing to receive, operate, modify or commercially exploit the Software or a Modified Work must obtain separate permission where required by this licence.

Publication of academic findings arising from permitted research is not prohibited merely because the research has some relationship with industry, provided that use of the Software itself was not principally undertaken as a commercial service or commercial product-development activity.

## 5. Commercial Use Is Prohibited

Without a separate written licence from the Copyright Holder, You may not use, reproduce, modify, distribute, host, provide access to, incorporate, bundle or otherwise exploit Covered Material or a Modified Work for Commercial Use.

Prohibited Commercial Use includes, without limitation:

a. selling the Software or a Modified Work;

b. charging for access to it;

c. incorporating it into a paid product, service, subscription or package;

d. providing it as part of an advertising-supported or sponsorship-supported product or service where You receive or control the commercial benefit;

e. using it to attract users or traffic to a monetised product or service where the Software forms a substantive part of that offering;

f. distributing it in return for advertising, sponsorship, affiliate or promotional revenue;

g. bundling it with commercial software, products or services;

h. providing it through a paid hosting, cloud, software-as-a-service or remotely accessed service;

i. using it to deliver paid consultancy or contract work;

j. using it to produce deliverables as part of commercial work;

k. using it for commercial research and development;

l. using it internally by or on behalf of a for-profit organisation where that use is intended to provide operational, research, development, financial, marketing or competitive benefit;

m. making it nominally available for no charge while obtaining commercial benefit from its use, availability or inclusion elsewhere;

n. using it as a substantive component of monetised media, advertising, promotional or sponsored content; or

o. incorporating it into another revenue-generating product or service.

**A price of zero does not make an activity non-commercial.**

Advertising-supported, sponsorship-supported or otherwise indirectly monetised use may therefore be Commercial Use even when users are not charged to access the Software.

## 6. General-Purpose Hosting Platforms

Use of a general-purpose repository, hosting, communications, publishing or video platform does not, by itself, make Your activity Commercial Use merely because the platform operator is a commercial business, displays general platform advertising, collects ordinary service data, or receives revenue independently of You.

For example, otherwise permitted publication of a non-commercial fork through a general-purpose source-code hosting service does not become Commercial Use solely because the hosting provider operates commercially.

Likewise, an educator does not breach this licence merely because a third-party video platform independently places general advertising around a non-monetised educational video.

This exception does not apply where You receive revenue, commercial benefit, sponsorship or other consideration connected with the Covered Material, or where the Covered Material forms part of a monetised offering under Your control.

## 7. Generated Outputs

The Copyright Holder does not claim ownership of a Generated Output solely because it was produced using the Software.

Generated Outputs may be used freely for the educational, academic, scientific, student and public-engagement purposes permitted by Section 4.

This includes use in academic papers, dissertations, theses, teaching materials, presentations, demonstrations, student portfolios, research websites and educational recordings.

Use of the Software to deliberately create Generated Outputs as part of a Commercial Use remains prohibited unless separately authorised.

For example, separate permission is required where the Software is deliberately used to generate material for a commercial advertising campaign, produce simulation material sold as part of a commercial product, regularly create content for a monetised commercial service, produce material for a paying commercial client, or generate assets as part of commercial product development.

A Generated Output does not become prohibited merely because it later appears in an incidental commercial context that was not contemplated when the permitted academic, educational or personal work was undertaken.

The Copyright Holder does not claim copyright in factual numerical results merely because those results were generated by the Software.

Where a Generated Output contains copyrightable project artwork, text, interface material or other protected content, rights in those incorporated elements remain unaffected.

Datasets, reference data, documentation, images or other materials distributed as part of the project may carry separate licence notices. Where they do, those notices govern the identified material.

## 8. Personal Use by Employees

An individual employed by a commercial organisation may use the Software privately for a genuinely personal Non-Commercial Use.

That exception does not apply where the use is undertaken for the employer, performed as part of employment duties, intended to benefit the employer's business, used in a commercial project, or otherwise connected with the employer's commercial activity.

